Subject: A few questions about reactivity (part 1: lists inside reactiveValues)
Message-ID: <LU9pWf0-tJE.lAWBuyiHAEEJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/shiny-discuss/c/LU9pWf0-tJE/m/lAWBuyiHAEEJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Given the following...

foo <- reactiveValues(alist=list(a=1,b=2),xlist=list(x=3,y=4));

...I found by trial and error (no wonder I kept getting endless loops) that:

foo$alist$a <- 42  will cause observe({foo$alist$b; ...}) to invalidate. In other words, only top-level objects inside foo are independent of each other but updating a sub-object is equivalent to updating the parent object.

I have several lists of session-specific variables and I want to keep the lists separate from each other (so I could, for example, iterate over foo$xlist without also iterating over foo$alist). Will any of the following work in a stable manner?

foo <- list(alist=reactiveValues(a=1,b=2),xlist=reactiveValues(x=3,y=4));
alist=reactiveValues(a=1,b=2); xlist=reactiveValues(x=3,y=4); foo<-list(alist,xlist);

...or is the recommended practice just to have each and every such list be a separate reactiveValues object at the top level of shinyServer()'s environment?

From u80defc9c4046a73b at groups.google.com  Thu Oct 10 17:49:01 2013
From: u80defc9c4046a73b at groups.google.com (Alex Bokov)
Date: Thu, 10 Oct 2013 17:49:01 +0000
Subject: A few questions about reactivity (part 2: assigning within an isolate statement)
Message-ID: <pXq5oz_H3eg._bsk_ISxiUkJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/shiny-discuss/c/pXq5oz_H3eg/m/_bsk_ISxiUkJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If we have...

foo <- reactiveValues(alist=list(a=1,b=2),blist=list(x=3,y=4));

You would expect that isolate(foo$alist <- as.list(1:3)) would prevent the invalidation of things that depend on foo$alist, analogously to how reading a variable wrapped in isolate prevents a dependency from being formed. Unfortunately, it does not.

My questions are:

* Is the effect of isolate(foo$alist <- as.list(1:3)) different in any way from the effect of foo$alist <- as.list(1:3) ?

* If there is a function which I want to update foo$alist silently (i.e. without triggering a cascade of invalidation) and there is another function where I do want the normal invalidation behavior on the same variable, is there a way to accomplish that without having to hunt down every observer that depends on foo$alist, and manually suspending it from within the first (silent) function?

