A little bit of potential progress, at least as far as what the user will experience... I found this post on SO, which suggested to check if input$name is NULL:
- http://stackoverflow.com/questions/13403627/rstudio-shiny-conditional-plot/13404013#13404013

If I add an if statement to my expenses function like this,

expenses <- reactive({

    members <- as.numeric(input$members)

    if(is.null(input$ind1)) {
      return(NULL)
    }

    else{ do interesting stuff }

  })

I get a blank plot from ggplot (the borders on the plot and facet headers are there, but the plot space is just white), and then it populates. After the first time, moving the sliders instantly changes the output, so it looks like the "shiny limbo land" only happens on the first run through. It's not ideal, but it *does* keep the red "Attempt to apply a non-function" message from appearing.

Is this the proper way? I think the real question is how one calls the input objects from a reactiveUI definition and has them available to lapply.

Take this shiny example for reactiveUI for example: http://rstudio.github.io/shiny/tutorial/#dynamic-ui

The code gives an example for reactive input, but doesn't do anything with the inputs. I fiddled with it to make it like my own and also see that the reactiveUI elements are not available the first time around. If you try to access them or do anything useful with them, it appears you get a brief error warning where your rendered output goes, which quickly populates with the actual result shortly after (once it runs again and actually creates the input object).

Frustrating bit, though I suppose it's experimental. Still looking for input on whether or not "this is just how things are" or if I'm not doing something correctly. How does one actually access the reactive elements created (I suppose the point in reactiveUI isn't *just* to make the form change, but to actually access the dynamically created results!)??

Thanks again,
John

